/* ---------- Gallery I ---------- */
a('g1-01',1,0,'photo','Wartime factory floor',{kind:'primary',f:['Ford assembly line - 1913.jpg'],creator:'Unknown (Ford Motor Company)',date:'1913',origin:'Highland Park, Michigan (Detroit area)',context:'Assembly-line industry was booming before and during World War I. This 1913 photograph shows Ford’s line, the kind of factory work that drew migrants north.',relevance:'Shows the industrial jobs that wartime labor shortages opened up in Northern cities.',cite:'foner-2019',
 blurb:'World War I cut off the flow of European immigrants just as American factories were booming with war orders. Employers who had rarely hired Black workers now sent recruiters south and printed train tickets in advance. Jobs in Chicago, Detroit, New York, and Pittsburgh paid far more than a tenant farmer could hope to earn in the cotton South. This photograph of a Ford assembly line (1913) shows the kind of industrial workplace that anchored the Northern job market. Black workers often got the hardest, dirtiest, and lowest-paid positions, but the wages were still a powerful pull.',prompt:'Why would a wartime labor shortage change who employers were willing to hire?',related:['g1-07','g1-08'],gq:[1]});
a('g1-02',1,0,'photo','Cotton and the boll weevil',{kind:'primary',f:['Information concerning the Mexican cotton boll weevil (1904) (19952024063).jpg'],creator:'Walter David Hunter, U.S. Dept. of Agriculture',date:'1904',origin:'USDA Farmers’ Bulletin No. 189',context:'The boll weevil crossed from Mexico into Texas in the 1890s and spread east across the cotton belt, reaching Georgia by the early 1920s.',relevance:'The insect destroyed cotton crops and the incomes of Southern farmers.',cite:'foner-2019',
 blurb:'This illustration comes from a 1904 federal bulletin that tried to teach farmers about a new threat. The boll weevil, a beetle that lays its eggs inside cotton bolls, moved east from Texas year after year. Where it arrived, harvests collapsed. Landowners cut their tenants’ acreage, merchants cut off credit, and families who already lived on the edge lost what little they had. The boll weevil did not cause the Great Migration alone, but it was a powerful economic push that made leaving the land look more sensible than staying.',prompt:'How could a single insect change the life of a family that did not own its land?',related:['g1-03'],gq:[1]});
a('g1-03',1,0,'document','Sharecropping and the debt trap',{kind:'primary',f:['Sharecropper Sam Williams with family members and laborers in cotton field LCCN98506914.jpg'],creator:'Photographer unknown (Library of Congress)',date:'c. 1900s',origin:'Southern United States',context:'After emancipation, many Black Southerners farmed land they did not own, paying landlords with a share of the crop. The ledger text in this pop-up is an <b>illustrative composite</b>, not a scan of one real ledger.',relevance:'Shows how tenant farming and sharecropping created debt traps.',cite:'foner-2019',
 doc:'ILLUSTRATIVE LEDGER (composite)\n\nCredit advanced at the plantation store:\n  Seed, tools, flour, cloth ......... $ 94.00\n  Interest and “commissary” markup ... $ 31.00\n\nCrop sold, tenant’s share ........ $ 88.00\n\nBalance carried forward as debt .... $ 37.00',
 blurb:'Sharecropping grew out of the broken promises of Reconstruction. A family farmed a landlord’s plot and paid with a share of the crop. Because the landlord also ran the store that supplied seed, food, and cloth on credit, the books at the end of the year often showed that the family still owed money. The landlord kept the accounts, set the prices, and could call the sheriff if a family tried to leave in debt. The result was a trap. Migration north was, for many, the only way out. The ledger shown here is a labeled composite that illustrates the pattern.',prompt:'Who controlled the numbers in the ledger, and why did that matter?',related:['g1-02','g1-04'],gq:[1]});
a('g1-04',1,1,'document','Poll taxes and literacy tests',{kind:'primary',q:'poll tax receipt',creator:'Southern state legislatures',date:'1890s–1900s',origin:'Southern states',context:'Mississippi’s 1890 constitution and similar laws across the South created barriers to voting. The text below is an <b>explanatory panel</b>, not an archival scan.',relevance:'Shows systemic disenfranchisement as a political push factor.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 doc:'HOW DISENFRANCHISEMENT WORKED\n\n• Poll tax: a fee due before voting, often months ahead, with receipts required at the polls.\n• Literacy test: a registrar chose a passage and decided who “understood” it.\n• The tools looked neutral on paper. Officials applied them unevenly, so most Black citizens were kept off the voting rolls.',
 blurb:'By the early 1900s, Southern states had written new constitutions and laws that kept Black men from voting without naming race. Poll taxes demanded cash from people who were paid in credit. Literacy tests let a white registrar decide who passed. Violence and intimidation backed up the paper rules. Because voting is how people change laws about schools, courts, and taxes, losing it meant losing the power to challenge segregation itself. In the North, Black citizens could still vote, and that political voice was part of the pull.',prompt:'Why is voting power connected to every other kind of equal treatment?',related:['g1-05','g1-07'],gq:[1]});
a('g1-05',1,1,'photo','Jim Crow in daily life',{kind:'primary',f:['JimCrowInDurhamNC.jpg'],creator:'Jack Delano, Farm Security Administration',date:'1940',origin:'Durham, North Carolina',context:'Taken in 1940, later than the 1920s, but it documents the signage system that governed Southern public life for decades.',relevance:'Jim Crow segregation shaped daily life across the South.',cite:'foner-2019',
 blurb:'Jim Crow laws and customs separated Black and white Southerners in railroad cars, schools, waiting rooms, restaurants, and cemeteries. “Separate but equal” was the legal doctrine since <i>Plessy v. Ferguson</i> (1896), but the facilities set aside for Black residents were almost always worse. This photograph from 1940 shows the signs that marked the rules, which stayed in place long after the 1920s. Signs reminded people of the color line many times a day. For families deciding whether to move north, the daily humiliations mattered as much as the wages.',prompt:'What does a sign do that a law on paper cannot?',related:['g1-04','g1-06'],gq:[1]});
a('g1-06',1,1,'evidence','Violence in the South',{kind:'secondary',ev:1,creator:'Museum curators',date:'1880s–1920s',origin:'Panel summarising secondary sources',context:'Text only. This museum does not show graphic images of lynching.',relevance:'Extreme racial violence, including lynchings, pushed people to leave.',cite:'foner-2019',sens:true,
 doc:'EVIDENCE PANEL\n\nLynching, the killing of a person by a mob without a trial, was used to terrorize Black communities. Many victims were never charged with any crime, and local officials seldom punished the killers. The threat of violence enforced Jim Crow and discouraged Black Southerners from claiming their rights.',
 blurb:'Racial violence was part of the system that held Jim Crow in place. Lynchings were public, and mobs often acted with the knowledge or help of local officials. Even people who were never attacked lived knowing it could happen to a relative or neighbor over an accusation, a business success, or a dispute over wages. Newspapers like the <i>Chicago Defender</i> spread word of safer places and urged readers to leave. This panel deliberately uses text instead of graphic images, out of respect for the victims and for visitors.',prompt:'Why might fear of violence outweigh the attachment people felt to their home communities?',related:['g1-05','g1-09'],gq:[1]});
a('g1-07',1,1,'photo','A Northern Black neighborhood',{kind:'primary',f:['Scott Arthur Family 1920.jpg'],creator:'The Chicago Defender / Chicago Commission on Race Relations',date:'1920',origin:'Chicago, Illinois',context:'The Scott Arthur family, shown in a 1920 image associated with Chicago reporting on Black life during the Great Migration.',relevance:'Better schools, voting rights, and growing urban communities pulled migrants north.',cite:'foner-2019',
 blurb:'Black families who arrived in Chicago, Harlem, Detroit, and Pittsburgh found real gains and real limits. They could vote, send children to better schools, join churches, unions, and newspapers, and start businesses that served growing neighborhoods such as Chicago’s South Side. But housing was restricted to a few crowded districts, rents were high, and many employers still shut them out. This photograph shows a family in Chicago in 1920. It reminds us that the migration was made up of ordinary households making a hopeful, risky choice.',prompt:'What might a family gain and risk by moving 800 miles from home?',related:['g1-01','g1-08'],gq:[1]});
a('g1-08',1,1,'map','Migration routes',{kind:'secondary',f:['During World War I there was a great migration north by southern Negroes - NARA - 559091.jpg'],map:true,creator:'Jacob Lawrence (Migration Series panel), via the National Archives',date:'1940–1941 (artwork)',origin:'New York',context:'A panel from Jacob Lawrence’s <i>Migration Series</i>. The schematic route map below is a simplified diagram, not a statistical map.',relevance:'Shows the main streams of movement from Southern origins to Northern destinations.',cite:'foner-2019',
 blurb:'Migrants followed railroad lines. People from Mississippi, Alabama, and Tennessee tended to ride the Illinois Central toward Chicago. Many from Georgia, the Carolinas, and Virginia went up the Atlantic coast to Washington, Philadelphia, and New York. Others reached Pittsburgh, Cleveland, and Detroit. Word spread through letters, relatives, and the <i>Chicago Defender</i>. This panel is by artist Jacob Lawrence, who painted the Migration Series in 1940–41. Try the interactive Migration Map in the Migration Map Room to compare destinations for yourself.',prompt:'Why would migrants follow rail lines and family networks instead of choosing a city at random?',related:['g1-07','g1-01'],gq:[1]});
a('g1-09',1,2,'map','The Red Summer of 1919',{kind:'primary',f:['Map of the rioting during the Washington DC race riot of 1919.jpg'],creator:'The Washington Times',date:'July 1919',origin:'Washington, D.C.',context:'A newspaper map of the disorder in Washington in July 1919, one of the Red Summer riots.',relevance:'More than 20 race riots erupted across the United States in 1919.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'Author James Weldon Johnson called the violent months of 1919 the “Red Summer.” More than 20 race riots broke out, from Washington, D.C., and Chicago to Omaha and Elaine, Arkansas. Black soldiers had just come home from the war and expected respect. White workers competed for jobs and housing as the economy lurched through demobilization. Many riots were started or driven by white mobs, but Black residents organized to defend themselves, and press coverage often blamed them. This map is from a newspaper report on the riot in the nation’s capital.',prompt:'Why might competition for jobs and housing raise tensions in crowded cities?',related:['g1-10','g1-11'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g1-10',1,2,'newspaper','Chicago Race Riot, 1919',{kind:'primary',f:['1919 Chicago Race Riot.jpg'],creator:'Grand Forks Herald (wire report)',date:'29 July 1919',origin:'Grand Forks, North Dakota',context:'A national wire story about the riot that began on July 27, 1919, after a Black teenager, Eugene Williams, drowned near a segregated Lake Michigan beach.',relevance:'Shows racial tension in a Northern destination city.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'Chicago’s Black population had roughly doubled during the war years, and neighborhoods near the stockyards and the lakefront were tense. On July 27, 1919, a Black teenager named Eugene Williams drifted across an invisible color line at a Lake Michigan beach and was struck by stones and drowned. Police refused to arrest the white man accused, and fighting spread for days. Thirty-eight people died and hundreds were injured. A state commission later studied the causes, including housing, jobs, and policing. This front page shows how far the story traveled.',prompt:'What does the Chicago riot show about the “promised land” of the North?',related:['g1-09','g1-07'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g1-11',1,2,'photo','Greenwood, Tulsa (1921)',{kind:'primary',f:['Ruins of the Tulsa Race Riot, 6-1-21 (14198065370).jpg'],sens:true,creator:'Alvin C. Krupnick Co. (SMU Central University Libraries)',date:'1 June 1921',origin:'Tulsa, Oklahoma',context:'Photograph of ruins in the Greenwood district after the massacre. Content note: this exhibit covers mass violence.',relevance:'White mobs burned the Black business district known as “Black Wall Street.”',cite:'staples-1999',
 blurb:'Greenwood was a thriving Black neighborhood in Tulsa, Oklahoma, with banks, hotels, newspapers, and shops, so prosperous that it was called “Black Wall Street.” After unproven allegations against a young Black man, white mobs, some of them deputized, attacked the district on May 31 and June 1, 1921. They burned homes and businesses, and up to 300 African Americans died. Thousands were left homeless and held in detention. The neighborhood was destroyed. For decades, the massacre was left out of many textbooks and local memory. This photograph shows some of what was left.',prompt:'Why might a community remember an event differently from the way official records do?',related:['g1-15','g1-09'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g1-12',1,3,'chart','The revived Klan',{kind:'secondary',f:['19151128AC Klan re-established.jpg'],chart:'klan',creator:'Atlanta Constitution',date:'28 November 1915',origin:'Atlanta, Georgia',context:'An Atlanta newspaper notice of the Klan’s re-establishment. The chart below shows the spread and peak size as estimated by historians.',relevance:'Re-founded at Stone Mountain in 1915, the Klan spread to the Midwest and West and peaked at about 4–5 million members.',cite:'foner-2019',
 blurb:'The first Ku Klux Klan was a Reconstruction-era terrorist group. A new organization was founded at Stone Mountain, Georgia, in 1915, inspired in part by the film <i>The Birth of a Nation</i>. By the mid-1920s it had spread well beyond the South into the Midwest and western cities, including Indiana, where it became a political force. Historians estimate membership at 4 to 5 million at its peak. The Klan sold itself as a respectable fraternal and civic group, used lobbying, rallies, and violence, and called its program “100% Americanism.” This exhibit presents it as evidence of how hate organized.',prompt:'Why would a hate group present itself as patriotic and respectable?',related:['g1-13','g1-14'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g1-13',1,3,'chart','Klan targets',{kind:'secondary',ev:1,chart:'klan-targets',creator:'Museum curators, from secondary sources',date:'1920s',origin:'Chart',context:'A diagram of the groups the 1920s Klan targeted.',relevance:'Targets widened from Black Americans to Catholics, Jews, immigrants, and modern or secular trends.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'The Reconstruction Klan focused on Black citizens and their white allies. The 1920s Klan widened its list. It attacked Roman Catholics, whom it called loyal to a foreign pope, along with Jews and recent immigrants, and it condemned bootlegging, evolution, jazz, and other signs of modern or secular life. These targets reflected the fears of a changing America: cities were growing, newcomers were arriving, and old Protestant dominance seemed threatened. The result was a movement that linked racism, nativism, and moral campaigns, and made them look like patriotism.',prompt:'What do the Klan’s many targets have in common?',related:['g1-12','g1-14'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g1-14',1,3,'photo','Klan march in Washington, D.C.',{kind:'primary',f:['Ku Klux Klan marching down Pennsylvania Ave., Washington, D.C LCCN2016888117.jpg'],creator:'Harris & Ewing',date:'mid-1920s',origin:'Pennsylvania Avenue, Washington, D.C.',context:'The Klan held large parades in the capital in 1925 and 1926.',relevance:'Public rallies and the slogan “100% Americanism.”',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'On Pennsylvania Avenue, thousands of Klan members marched openly in robes past the Capitol. Parades like these showed that the organization felt confident and was accepted in many communities. Marches were designed to look orderly and patriotic, with flags and bands. Opponents saw them as intimidation. The Klan’s power shrank in the late 1920s after scandals and internal conflict, including the conviction of Indiana leader D. C. Stephenson for murder in 1925, but its ideas outlived its membership numbers. This photograph is presented as historical evidence with context, not as celebration.',prompt:'What message does a mass march in the capital send to the public and to lawmakers?',related:['g1-12','g1-13'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g1-15',1,3,'article','“Unearthing a Riot” (Brent Staples)',{kind:'secondary',creator:'Brent Staples',date:'1999',origin:'The New York Times Magazine',context:'Candidate secondary source for the Tulsa room. <b>Confirm exactly what the article covers before quoting it</b>; this card summarizes it in general terms only.',relevance:'A modern journalist’s account of how the Tulsa riot was remembered and rediscovered.',cite:'staples-1999',
 blurb:'Brent Staples wrote about the Tulsa race riot of 1921 for <i>The New York Times Magazine</i> in 1999, at a time when historians and state officials were digging back into a story that had been buried for generations. As a secondary source, it helps you ask how events fade from public memory and how they come back. A good historian checks a secondary source against primary evidence such as photographs, survivor accounts, and records. Use this article as a guide, not as final proof, and read the bibliography entry to find it.',prompt:'How does a modern article differ from a photograph taken in 1921 as evidence?',related:['g1-11'],gq:[2]});

/* ---------- Gallery II ---------- */
a('g2-01',2,0,'newspaper','The Bolshevik Revolution, 1917',{kind:'secondary',f:['The Russian Bolshevik Revolution (1921) (14740439886).jpg'],creator:'Edward A. Ross (book illustration)',date:'1921',origin:'<i>The Russian Bolshevik Revolution</i> (book)',context:'A book illustration about the revolution. In 1917–19 American newspapers covered Russia daily and often alarmingly.',relevance:'The First Red Scare (1919–20) was fueled by the 1917 revolution in Russia.',cite:'foner-2019',
 blurb:'In 1917 Lenin’s Bolsheviks seized power in Russia and withdrew from the war. Many Americans feared that radical revolution could cross borders. The fear deepened in 1919, when strikes spread across the United States and Communist parties formed here. Newspapers printed lurid headlines about “Reds.” Real Russian events were mixed with rumors and exaggeration, and politicians used them to cast doubt on labor organizers, immigrants, and critics of the war. This was the foundation of the First Red Scare, which peaked in 1919 and 1920.',prompt:'How can distant events change politics at home?',related:['g2-02'],gq:[2]});
a('g2-02',2,0,'newspaper','Anarchist bombing coverage',{kind:'primary',f:['Wall Street bombing 1920 wreckage.jpg'],creator:'Unknown',date:'16 September 1920',origin:'Wall Street, New York City',context:'Wreckage after a bomb exploded outside the J. P. Morgan bank, killing dozens. In 1919 a series of mail bombs and an explosion at Attorney General Palmer’s house had already alarmed the nation.',relevance:'High-profile bombings stoked fear of anarchists.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'In spring and June 1919, mail bombs and explosions targeted officials and businessmen, including Attorney General A. Mitchell Palmer’s home. In September 1920 a horse-drawn wagon exploded on Wall Street, killing dozens of people. The perpetrators were never conclusively identified, but many Americans blamed anarchists and foreign radicals. Newspaper coverage and public anger made it easier for officials to defend sweeping crackdowns. The bombings were real, and so were the fears, but historians note that the government’s response went far beyond the actual threat.',prompt:'How do real acts of violence shape public support for harsh responses?',related:['g2-04','g2-05'],gq:[2]});
a('g2-03',2,0,'document','Espionage and Sedition Acts',{kind:'primary',f:['Eugene Debs speech in Canton.jpg'],creator:'James V. McCann (photograph); U.S. Congress (laws)',date:'1917 and 1918 (photo: June 1918)',origin:'Washington, D.C. / Canton, Ohio',context:'Socialist leader Eugene V. Debs speaking in Canton, Ohio, in June 1918. He was arrested for that speech and sentenced to ten years under the Espionage Act.',relevance:'Wartime laws restricting speech created civil-liberties violations.',cite:'foner-2019',
 doc:'IN PLAIN LANGUAGE\n\nEspionage Act (1917): made it a crime to interfere with the military draft or to help the nation’s enemies.\nSedition Act (1918): made it a crime to use “disloyal, profane, scurrilous, or abusive language” about the government, flag, or military.',
 blurb:'To fight the war and keep public support, Congress passed the Espionage Act in 1917 and the Sedition Act in 1918. They were used against newspapers, labor radicals, and war critics. Thousands were prosecuted, and mail privileges were denied to publications that opposed the war. Eugene V. Debs, a Socialist who ran for president, was sentenced to ten years for an anti-war speech. The Supreme Court upheld such convictions in 1919. These laws and the climate around them set the stage for the postwar Palmer Raids and for the founding of the ACLU.',prompt:'Where is the line between criticizing the government and endangering the nation?',related:['g2-04','g2-07'],gq:[1]});
a('g2-04',2,1,'photo','The Palmer Raids',{kind:'primary',f:['Radicals awaiting deportation.jpg'],creator:'Unknown',date:'1919–1920',origin:'United States',context:'Men detained as suspected radicals awaiting deportation. The raids peaked in November 1919 and January 1920.',relevance:'Warrantless mass arrests and deportations of suspected radicals, without due process.',cite:'foner-2019',
 blurb:'On the orders of Attorney General A. Mitchell Palmer, Justice Department agents raided union halls, meeting rooms, and homes in dozens of cities, mostly in November 1919 and January 1920. Thousands of people were arrested, often without warrants, and held without lawyers. Many were immigrants, and most were not dangerous. Hundreds were deported. Federal judges, including a Labor Department official, Louis F. Post, later cancelled many cases. The raids became a symbol of what happens when fear overrides due process, the idea that government must follow fair procedures before taking away liberty.',prompt:'What protections does “due process” give a person who is arrested?',related:['g2-05','g2-10'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g2-05',2,1,'portrait','Attorney General A. Mitchell Palmer',{kind:'primary',f:['A. Mitchell Palmer cph.3a02984.jpg'],creator:'Underwood & Underwood',date:'c. 1919',origin:'Washington, D.C.',context:'Palmer served as Attorney General from 1919 to 1921 under President Wilson. His home was bombed on June 2, 1919.',relevance:'Palmer orchestrated the raids.',cite:'foner-2019',
 blurb:'A. Mitchell Palmer was a Pennsylvania Quaker and reform-minded politician who became Attorney General in 1919. After the bomb attack on his own home he created a new Radical Division within the Justice Department, led by young J. Edgar Hoover, to collect files on suspected radicals. Palmer claimed that a revolution was imminent and that May Day 1920 would be a flashpoint. When May 1 passed without incident, his predictions looked foolish, and public support for the raids faded. His career never recovered. His statements show how a public official can fan fear to justify extraordinary powers.',prompt:'How should we judge public statements when later events prove them wrong?',related:['g2-04','g2-10'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g2-06',2,3,'portrait','The ACLU founders',{kind:'secondary',f:['Roger Baldwin 1923.jpg','Crystal Catherine Eastman in 1915.jpg','Jane Addams - Bain News Service.jpg'],captions:['Roger Baldwin','Crystal Eastman','Jane Addams'],creator:'Various (Strauss; Bain News Service)',date:'1915–1923',origin:'United States',context:'Left to right: Roger Baldwin, Crystal Eastman, and Jane Addams. All three were involved in the National Civil Liberties Bureau and the new ACLU.',relevance:'Key founders included Roger Baldwin, Crystal Eastman, and Jane Addams.',cite:'foner-2019',
 blurb:'The ACLU grew out of the National Civil Liberties Bureau, created in 1917 to defend conscientious objectors. Roger Baldwin became its director and guided the new organization after 1920. Crystal Eastman, a lawyer, feminist, and labor reformer, co-founded the bureau with him. Jane Addams, the Nobel Peace Prize winner and founder of Hull-House in Chicago, lent her name and her moral weight. Together they argued that rights belong to everyone, including unpopular people, and that a nation is judged by how it treats those it fears.',prompt:'Why was it important that the founders came from different backgrounds?',related:['g2-07','g2-08'],gq:[1]});
a('g2-07',2,3,'document','The ACLU’s mission, 1920',{kind:'primary',creator:'American Civil Liberties Union',date:'January 1920',origin:'New York',context:'The ACLU was formed on January 19, 1920. The text below paraphrases its purpose; it is not a verbatim quote of the founding document.',relevance:'Defends First Amendment speech, assembly, and press, and due process, regardless of the accused’s beliefs.',cite:'foner-2019',
 doc:'MISSION (paraphrased)\n\n• Defend freedom of speech, assembly, and the press.\n• Defend the right to due process of law.\n• Defend these rights for everyone, whatever their political beliefs.',
 blurb:'The ACLU’s most radical idea was neutrality about beliefs. It would defend a Socialist, a labor organizer, or a Klan member’s right to speak, not because it agreed, but because rights that can be taken from one group are not safe for any group. The organization focused on the First Amendment guarantees of speech, assembly, and press, and on the fair procedures in the Bill of Rights. Its early work was modest in size, with a small office, a few lawyers, and volunteers, but it created a lasting model: citizens organizing to hold government to the Constitution.',prompt:'Why defend rights for people whose beliefs you reject?',related:['g2-06','g2-08'],gq:[1]});
a('g2-08',2,3,'photo','The Scopes Trial, 1925',{kind:'primary',f:['W. J. Bryan at Scopes Trial, 1925 photo by United News Pictures.jpg'],creator:'United News Pictures',date:'July 1925',origin:'Dayton, Tennessee',context:'William Jennings Bryan at the trial of teacher John T. Scopes, who was charged with teaching evolution in violation of Tennessee’s Butler Act.',relevance:'The ACLU provided legal defense in this high-profile case.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'Tennessee made it illegal to teach human evolution in public schools. The ACLU announced that it would defend any teacher who challenged the law, and a young Dayton teacher, John Scopes, agreed to be the test case. Clarence Darrow led the defense, and William Jennings Bryan helped the prosecution. Scopes was convicted and fined $100, a verdict later overturned on a technicality. The trial, one of the first major media events, brought national attention to the ACLU and to the clash between modern science and traditional faith in the 1920s.',prompt:'Why might the ACLU choose to support a test case?',related:['g2-09','g2-06'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g2-09',2,3,'document','Labor organizers and anti-war dissidents',{kind:'primary',q:'Industrial Workers of the World trial 1918',creator:'ACLU case summaries',date:'1920s',origin:'Various',context:'A summary panel of the types of cases the early ACLU took. These are <b>illustrative summaries</b>, not individual archival files.',relevance:'The ACLU also defended labor organizers and war protesters.',cite:'foner-2019',
 doc:'EARLY CASE TYPES\n\n• Striking workers arrested for picketing or speaking.\n• Opponents of the war punished under the Espionage and Sedition Acts.\n• Immigrants threatened with deportation for their beliefs.',
 blurb:'Free speech cases in the early 1920s were rarely about artists or authors. They were about striking workers, soapbox speakers, and immigrants accused of radical ideas. The ACLU provided lawyers, raised bail, and publicized abuses by police and vigilantes. It also challenged the Espionage Act convictions that had put war critics in prison. In these cases the organization showed that civil liberties and labor rights were closely linked: when a union could be called a Bolshevik plot, even a peaceful strike could lead to arrest.',prompt:'How were labor rights and free speech connected in the 1920s?',related:['g2-03','g2-08'],gq:[1]});
a('g2-10',2,2,'document','Government arguments and defense arguments',{kind:'primary',creator:'Museum curators (composite)',date:'1919–1920',origin:'Documented patterns of the raids',context:'Paired arguments used in the Civil-Liberties Decision Simulation. They are <b>composites</b> based on documented positions on each side.',relevance:'Side-by-side positions for the simulation.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 doc:'GOVERNMENT SAYS\n• Revolutionary groups threaten public safety.\n• Speed is vital; waiting for warrants lets suspects escape.\n• Non-citizens can be removed from the country.\n\nDEFENSE SAYS\n• Membership in a group is not a crime.\n• The Fourth and Fifth Amendments require warrants and fair hearings.\n• Deportation without a hearing is punishment without trial.',
 blurb:'Every civil-liberties dispute pits real concerns against real principles. Officials in 1919 argued that bombings and strikes proved a revolutionary threat, and that waiting for warrants and trials would give radicals time to act. Their critics replied that the Constitution does not disappear in emergencies, that guilt must be proved person by person, and that arrests based on membership or ideas are exactly what the Bill of Rights was designed to stop. Read both sides carefully, then try the Civil-Liberties Decision Simulation to see how you would decide.',prompt:'Which side’s argument is stronger on each case, and why?',related:['g2-04','g2-05'],gq:[1,2]});

/* ---------- Gallery III ---------- */
a('g3-01',3,0,'poster','Wartime propaganda poster',{kind:'primary',f:['"Beat back the HUN with LIBERTY BONDS." - NARA - 512638.jpg'],creator:'U.S. government (Liberty Loan poster)',date:'1918',origin:'United States',context:'A Liberty Loan poster of the kind the government and the Committee on Public Information used to shape opinion.',relevance:'The Committee on Public Information fostered anti-German and anti-foreigner sentiment.',cite:'foner-2019',
 blurb:'To win public support, the Wilson administration created the Committee on Public Information (CPI) in 1917. It published pamphlets, organized speakers, and promoted posters and films. Posters such as this one portrayed the enemy as a monstrous “Hun.” The campaigns united many Americans behind the war, but they also encouraged suspicion of anyone with a German name or accent. German-language newspapers were closed, German music was banned in some cities, and sauerkraut was renamed “liberty cabbage.” After the war, the same suspicion was redirected at other foreigners.',prompt:'How can a poster turn a political enemy into a stereotype?',related:['g3-02','g3-03'],gq:[2]});
a('g3-02',3,0,'newspaper','Anti-German hostility',{kind:'primary',q:'anti-German World War I newspaper 1918',creator:'Museum curators (summary)',date:'1917–1919',origin:'United States',context:'Summary panel in place of a single clipping. Replace with an archival clipping if you find one in Chronicling America.',relevance:'Wartime hostility grew into nativism after the war.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 doc:'WHAT WARTIME HOSTILITY LOOKED LIKE\n\n• Schools dropped German-language classes.\n• German-language newspapers were censored or closed.\n• Some German Americans were attacked, and in 1918 Robert Prager was lynched in Illinois.\n• Many families anglicized their names.',
 blurb:'When the United States entered the war in 1917, Germans were the largest immigrant group descended from a single country. They suddenly found their loyalty questioned. Some towns burned German books, schools dropped the language, and mobs attacked people accused of disloyalty. The most extreme case was the lynching of Robert Prager in Collinsville, Illinois, in 1918. After the war the hostility did not disappear, but turned into a broader suspicion of foreigners, and the labels “hyphenated American” and “100% American” became common.',prompt:'How does hostility toward one group spread to others?',related:['g3-01','g3-04'],gq:[2]});
a('g3-03',3,0,'cartoon','Immigration political cartoon',{kind:'primary',f:['Illus. for article "an alien anti-dumping bill" in The Literary Digest, May 7, 1921, p. 13, reprinting a cartoon by Hallhan for Providence Evening Bulletin, showing funnel bridging Atlantic LCCN2007680185.jpg'],creator:'Hallhan, <i>Providence Evening Bulletin</i> (reprinted in <i>The Literary Digest</i>)',date:'7 May 1921',origin:'Providence, Rhode Island',context:'A cartoon about an “alien anti-dumping bill,” a title that treats immigrants as goods being dumped.',relevance:'Shows public fear of immigrants as one cartoonist saw it.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 hot:[['The funnel across the ocean','Europe is drawn as pouring people directly into the United States.'],['The bill’s title','Calling it an “anti-dumping” bill compares people to cheap imported goods.']],
 blurb:'Political cartoons compress an argument into a picture. This 1921 cartoon from a Providence paper was reprinted in a national magazine as Congress debated the first quota law. The image of a funnel connecting Europe to America suggests an uncontrollable flood, while the title of the “alien anti-dumping bill” borrows the language of trade protection and applies it to human beings. Ask who made it, who it was meant to persuade, and which details exaggerate. A cartoon is a primary source about opinion, not a neutral record of facts.',prompt:'Whose side was the cartoonist on, and which details tell you?',related:['g3-06','g3-05'],gq:[2]});
a('g3-04',3,0,'newspaper','Red Scare headline',{kind:'primary',f:['Palatka Daily News November 8 1919.jpg'],creator:'<i>Palatka Daily News</i>',date:'8 November 1919',origin:'Palatka, Florida',context:'A front page from the day after the first big Justice Department raids on radical groups.',relevance:'Fear of anarchists and communists, and of foreign political influence.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'On November 7, 1919, the second anniversary of the Bolshevik Revolution, federal agents raided offices of the Union of Russian Workers in a dozen cities. The next day, small-town newspapers across the country carried the story prominently. Headlines painted a picture of a hidden army of dangerous foreign plotters. The coverage helped win public approval for the raids and, in turn, the larger crackdown of January 1920. Reading old front pages shows how information, rumor, and fear traveled together.',prompt:'What impression would a reader in a small Florida town get from this front page?',related:['g3-02','g4-10'],gq:[2]});
a('g3-05',3,0,'document','Nativist speech excerpt',{kind:'primary',creator:'Calvin Coolidge',date:'1921',origin:'<i>Good Housekeeping</i>',context:'Coolidge was Vice President when he wrote on immigration. The phrase is widely quoted as an example of the political language of restriction.',relevance:'Political language for restriction.',cite:'foner-2019',
 doc:'EXCERPT (brief)\n\n“America must be kept American.”\n— Calvin Coolidge, 1921',
 blurb:'Restriction was not only about numbers. It was also about a vision of who counted as American. Calvin Coolidge, then Vice President and soon president, used a short phrase that became a slogan for the movement: America must be kept American. Supporters of restriction also spoke of protecting wages, preserving “Nordic” stock, and defending the nation against radicals. Scholars today read these speeches as evidence of how scientific-sounding racism and economic worry blended together. As you read, ask who is included in “American.”',prompt:'Who is included in “American” in this language, and who is left out?',related:['g3-06','g3-07'],gq:[2]});
a('g3-06',3,2,'document','Emergency Quota Act of 1921',{kind:'primary',q:'Emergency Quota Act 1921',creator:'U.S. Congress',date:'19 May 1921',origin:'Washington, D.C.',context:'The first U.S. law to limit European immigration by nationality.',relevance:'Capped annual immigration. Quotas set at 3% of each nationality’s foreign-born population in the 1910 Census.',cite:'foner-2019',
 doc:'KEY FEATURES\n\n• Quota: 3% of each nationality’s foreign-born population\n• Census baseline: 1910\n• Effect: capped total annual immigration and set nationality quotas',
 blurb:'Before 1921, the United States had few numerical limits on European immigration. The Emergency Quota Act changed that. It allowed each nationality to send 3 percent of the number of its people living in the United States in the 1910 Census. Because earlier waves from Northern and Western Europe were larger in 1910, they received the biggest shares, but the law still reduced arrivals from Southern and Eastern Europe sharply. It was meant to be temporary, and it opened the way to a permanent system. Try the Quota Calculator to see how a choice of baseline can change who benefits.',prompt:'Why was the choice of a baseline year such a powerful decision?',related:['g3-07','g3-08'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g3-07',3,2,'newspaper','National Origins Act of 1924',{kind:'primary',f:['America of the melting pot comes to end.png'],creator:'<i>The New York Times</i>',date:'1924',origin:'New York',context:'A newspaper headline marking the passage of the Immigration Act of 1924 (the Johnson–Reed Act).',relevance:'Lowered the quota to 2% and moved the baseline to the 1890 Census.',cite:'foner-2019',
 doc:'KEY FEATURES\n\n• Quota: 2% of each nationality’s foreign-born population\n• Census baseline: 1890\n• Barred nearly all Asian immigration',
 blurb:'The Immigration Act of 1924 made the system stricter and more openly aimed at particular groups. The quota fell to 2 percent, and the baseline moved back to the 1890 Census, before large numbers of Italians, Poles, Jews, and others had arrived. The law also barred immigrants who were “ineligible for citizenship,” which in practice meant nearly all Asians. As the headline in <i>The New York Times</i> put it, the “melting pot” era was ending. The national-origins system stayed, with changes, until 1965.',prompt:'What did moving the baseline from 1910 to 1890 accomplish?',related:['g3-06','g3-09'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g3-08',3,2,'chart','Quota comparison',{kind:'secondary',ev:1,chart:'quota',creator:'Museum curators, from the text of the laws',date:'1921 and 1924',origin:'Chart',context:'A comparison of the two laws’ quota percentages and baselines.',relevance:'Shows who each law favored and who it curtailed.',cite:'foner-2019',
 blurb:'Placing the two laws side by side makes the pattern clear. The 1921 law used a 3 percent rate and the 1910 Census. The 1924 law used a 2 percent rate and the 1890 Census, which sharply cut the Southern and Eastern European share while keeping a generous share for Britain, Ireland, and Germany. For Asians, the law did not use quotas at all, but a flat ban. The numbers sound like mathematics, but they were political choices about who should be allowed to become American.',prompt:'Which groups gain and which lose when the baseline moves earlier?',related:['g3-06','g3-07'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g3-09',3,2,'chart','Old versus New Immigrants',{kind:'secondary',ev:1,chart:'oldnew',creator:'Museum curators, from secondary sources',date:'1924',origin:'Chart',context:'A chart of the groups the 1924 law favored and curtailed.',relevance:'The 1924 law favored Northern and Western Europeans, curtailed Southern and Eastern Europeans (mostly Catholic or Jewish), and barred Asian immigration.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'Supporters of restriction divided immigrants into “Old Immigrants,” from Britain, Ireland, Germany, and Scandinavia, and “New Immigrants,” from Italy, Poland, Russia, Greece, and the Balkans. The second group was often Catholic or Jewish, and was described as harder to assimilate, a claim historians now reject. The 1924 law favored the first group and sharply reduced the second. It also shut out Asian immigrants completely. This chart shows the pattern, and the line between “Old” and “New” was drawn by prejudice, not by any fixed rule.',prompt:'Was the difference between “Old” and “New” immigrants a fact or a judgment?',related:['g3-07','g3-08'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g3-10',3,3,'photo','Sacco and Vanzetti',{kind:'primary',f:['Sacvan.jpg'],creator:'Unknown',date:'c. 1921–1927',origin:'Massachusetts',context:'Nicola Sacco and Bartolomeo Vanzetti, Italian immigrants and self-described anarchists, in custody.',relevance:'Convicted of robbery and murder in Massachusetts.',cite:'foner-2019',
 quote:'Audio clips are not included. Use the Radio Room for recordings available with clear rights.',
 blurb:'Nicola Sacco, a shoemaker, and Bartolomeo Vanzetti, a fish peddler, were Italian immigrants and self-proclaimed anarchists. They were arrested in May 1920 and charged with a payroll robbery and murder in South Braintree, Massachusetts. They were tried in 1921 before Judge Webster Thayer, who was reported to have made hostile remarks about them outside court. Both were convicted. Their case became an international cause. Supporters believed they were convicted because of their ethnicity and politics, and their final statements still move readers today.',prompt:'How might a defendant’s politics or background influence a jury?',related:['g3-11','g3-04'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g3-11',3,3,'evidence','Trial evidence panel',{kind:'secondary',ev:1,creator:'Museum curators, from secondary sources',date:'1921–1927',origin:'Dedham, Massachusetts',context:'A summary of the weaknesses historians and contemporaries pointed out.',relevance:'Weak physical evidence and unreliable witnesses. Both were executed in 1927. The trial showed judicial bias, nativism, and fear of radical ideas.',cite:'foner-2019',
 doc:'EVIDENCE PANEL\n\n• Physical evidence was weak and disputed.\n• Several eyewitnesses contradicted each other.\n• The prosecution stressed the defendants’ radicalism and their draft evasion.\n• Both men were executed on August 23, 1927.',
 blurb:'The case against Sacco and Vanzetti rested on evidence that historians have long described as weak. Ballistics claims were disputed, witnesses disagreed about what they saw, and the defendants had alibis supported by friends and fellow Italians, whom the court discounted. The prosecutor spent time on their radical beliefs and their avoidance of the draft. A review panel appointed by the governor upheld the verdict, and both men were executed in 1927. In 1977, Massachusetts Governor Michael Dukakis declared that they had not received a fair trial. The case exposed judicial bias, nativism, and fear of radical ideology.',prompt:'Which parts of the trial were about the crime, and which were about the defendants’ identity?',related:['g3-10'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g3-12',3,3,'photo','Boston Police Strike',{kind:'primary',f:['Flag raising ceremony on Boston Common, First Motor Corps, Mass. Nat. Guard. Poss. Police strike of 1919 - DPLA - a2f502dfd74acdee08a7a057eabc3231.jpg'],creator:'Unknown (Digital Public Library of America)',date:'1919',origin:'Boston Common, Massachusetts',context:'Photo associated with the Massachusetts National Guard during the 1919 police strike period.',relevance:'Strikes were portrayed in the media as communist plots.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'In September 1919, Boston police walked out after the department refused to recognize their union and after pay failed to keep up with inflation. Looting and disorder followed, and the state called in the National Guard. Governor Calvin Coolidge became a national hero by declaring that there was no right to strike against the public safety. Newspapers described the strike as a step toward Bolshevism, though the strikers wanted better pay and conditions. The police who struck were fired and replaced. The episode hardened public opposition to unions in the postwar months.',prompt:'Why might a strike be described as a “plot” instead of a labor dispute?',related:['g3-13','g3-14'],gq:[2]});
a('g3-13',3,3,'photo','Seattle General Strike',{kind:'primary',f:['Seattle General Strike 1919 Participants Leaving Shipyard.jpg'],creator:'Webster & Stevens',date:'February 1919',origin:'Seattle, Washington',context:'Workers leaving a shipyard during the Seattle General Strike.',relevance:'Labor unrest tied to communist fear.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'In February 1919, roughly 65,000 Seattle workers stopped work in sympathy with striking shipyard workers. For five days, strike committees ran essential services such as milk delivery and garbage collection. The strike ended peacefully, but Mayor Ole Hanson claimed publicly that it was an attempt at revolution and became a national figure. The incident influenced how other strikes in 1919 were reported, and it helped convince many Americans that organized labor and communism were linked. This image shows workers leaving a shipyard at the start of the walkout.',prompt:'Do you think the label “revolution” fit what happened in Seattle?',related:['g3-12','g3-14'],gq:[2]});
a('g3-14',3,3,'ad','The American Plan',{kind:'primary',q:'American Plan open shop',creator:'Business associations (summary)',date:'1920–1921',origin:'United States',context:'Summary panel of the open-shop campaign. Replace with an archival advertisement if you find one.',relevance:'Business groups promoted open-shop policies that suppressed union membership.',cite:'foner-2019',
 doc:'THE AMERICAN PLAN (summary)\n\n• Employers promoted “open shops” where unions had no formal role.\n• Campaigns described unions as un-American.\n• Welfare programs and company unions were offered as alternatives.',
 blurb:'After the strikes of 1919, business groups launched a campaign for the “American Plan,” a name that linked open-shop employment with patriotism. Newspaper advertisements and speeches described union shops as controlled by outsiders and radicals. Employers fired organizers, used spies and blacklists, and set up company-run unions. Courts and government agencies mostly sided with employers during the decade. The campaign was effective: union membership fell sharply during the 1920s, as the next chart shows.',prompt:'Why might a business group label its policy the “American” Plan?',related:['g3-13','g3-15'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g3-15',3,3,'chart','Union enrollment',{kind:'secondary',ev:1,chart:'union',creator:'Museum curators; approximate figures from labor-history estimates',date:'1920–1930',origin:'Chart',context:'Approximate national union membership. Exact counts differ by source; check a labor-history reference such as Foner or Henretta before quoting precise numbers.',relevance:'Union membership declined sharply during the decade.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'Estimates vary by source, but the direction is clear: unions lost ground after their wartime peak. Membership stood at roughly 5 million in 1920 and fell to roughly 3.5 million by the end of the decade. Open-shop drives, court rulings, a weak economy in 1920–21, and public suspicion all played a part. The decline shows how fear of radicalism helped reshape the economy, not just the courtroom. The chart is approximate and labeled as such; look up exact figures in the cited textbooks.',prompt:'How does fear of radicals connect to the decline of unions?',related:['g3-14','g3-12'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g3-16',3,0,'film','Nosferatu (student parody)',{kind:'secondary',parody:1,video:'assets/video/nosferatu-parody.mp4',poster:'assets/video/nosferatu-poster.jpg',creator:'Anish and Aarav (filmed); Anish (edited), as credited on the film’s title card',date:'2026',origin:'The Roaring Society project team',context:'A short black-and-white <b>parody of <i>Nosferatu</i></b> (1922), the German silent horror film. It was made by students for this museum and is <b>not</b> a 1920s source.',relevance:'A modern, playful reading of how the fears of the early 1920s showed up on screen: the stranger from far away who arrives bringing disease and danger.',mla:'Anish, and Aarav, filmers. Anish, editor. <i>Nosferatu</i> (student parody). The Roaring Society, 2026.',
 blurb:'This short film is a <b>parody of <i>Nosferatu</i></b> (1922), the German silent horror classic directed by F. W. Murnau. Murnau’s film was an unauthorized retelling of Bram Stoker’s <i>Dracula</i>. Stoker’s widow sued, and a court ordered copies destroyed, but some survived, and the film reached American theaters in 1929. Its monster, Count Orlok, is an outsider who arrives from the East with rats and plague, and many critics read him as a picture of postwar fear. That matches this room. After a world war, the 1918 flu, and the Russian Revolution, many Americans treated foreigners and radical ideas as contagions to be kept out, the same mood behind the Red Scare and the immigration quotas. The parody borrows Orlok’s famous doorway pose and moonlit castle for laughs. Treat it as a modern secondary source: it shows how people today re-use the look of the 1920s, not what people thought at the time.',prompt:'How does a modern parody help you picture the fears of the 1920s, and what can it not tell you?',related:['g3-03','g3-04']});

/* ---------- Gallery IV ---------- */
a('g4-01',4,0,'portrait','Benjamin Gitlow',{kind:'primary',f:['Gitlow-benjamin-1928.jpg'],creator:'Unknown',date:'1928',origin:'United States',context:'Photograph of Benjamin Gitlow, a leader of the Socialist Party’s left wing and later a founder of the Communist Party USA.',relevance:'A socialist leader.',cite:'gitlow-1925',
 blurb:'Benjamin Gitlow was a New York Socialist who became a leader of the party’s left wing in 1919. He was elected to the state assembly in 1918 and helped publish a journal called <i>The Revolutionary Age</i>. When the Socialist Party split, he joined the new Communist Party. He was arrested in November 1919 during the Red Scare. He later served time in Sing Sing, was pardoned, and eventually left the Communist Party and turned against it. His case matters not for his politics, but for what the Court said about state power.',prompt:'Does a person’s politics matter to the constitutional question in his case?',related:['g4-02','g4-04'],gq:[1]});
a('g4-02',4,0,'document','The Left Wing Manifesto',{kind:'primary',q:'Revolutionary Age 1919 Left Wing Manifesto',creator:'Left Wing Section of the Socialist Party (published by Gitlow)',date:'July 1919',origin:'<i>The Revolutionary Age</i>, New York',context:'The text below summarizes the document; it is not a reproduction of the full manifesto.',relevance:'Gitlow published it. It advocated overthrowing the government.',cite:'gitlow-1925',
 doc:'SUMMARY\n\nThe Manifesto called for a workers’ revolution and the replacement of the existing government with a “dictatorship of the proletariat.” It criticized moderate socialism and praised the Russian Revolution.',
 blurb:'The Left Wing Manifesto was published in 1919 in a radical newspaper that Gitlow helped run. It urged workers to prepare for a revolution and replace the existing government with a government of workers. It did not describe any plan to act immediately, and the evidence showed that the paper circulated only modestly. But New York prosecutors argued that the language itself was a crime under state law, whether or not anyone acted on it. That raised the central question in the case: can the state punish ideas that urge radical change, or only actions that cause immediate danger?',prompt:'Is advocating a revolution the same as starting one?',related:['g4-03','g4-07'],gq:[1]});
a('g4-03',4,1,'document','New York Criminal Anarchy Law',{kind:'primary',creator:'New York State Legislature',date:'1902',origin:'Albany, New York',context:'Passed after the assassination of President McKinley by an anarchist in 1901. The summary below is paraphrased.',relevance:'The statute under which he was convicted.',cite:'gitlow-1925',
 doc:'IN PLAIN LANGUAGE\n\nThe law made it a crime to advocate, by speech or writing, the doctrine that organized government should be overthrown by force, violence, or unlawful means.',
 blurb:'New York passed its Criminal Anarchy Law in 1902, a year after anarchist Leon Czolgosz assassinated President William McKinley. It made it a felony to advocate the overthrow of organized government by force or violence. The statute punished speech itself, not a plan or a deed. Few people were prosecuted under it until the Red Scare of 1919 and 1920. Gitlow’s case was the first major challenge to such a law to reach the Supreme Court, and the Court had to decide whether the state could ban a doctrine as such.',prompt:'Does a law against “advocating” something punish thought or action?',related:['g4-02','g4-07'],gq:[1]});
a('g4-04',4,1,'newspaper','Arrest and conviction',{kind:'primary',ev:0,q:'Gitlow 1920 arrest conviction',creator:'Museum curators, from court records and secondary sources',date:'1919–1925',origin:'New York to Washington, D.C.',context:'Timeline summary of the case.',relevance:'How the case moved from arrest to the Supreme Court.',cite:'gitlow-1925',
 doc:'TIMELINE\n\nNov 1919  Gitlow arrested in the Red Scare raids.\nFeb 1920  Convicted under the Criminal Anarchy Law.\n1922      New York appeals court upholds conviction.\n1923–25  U.S. Supreme Court hears the case.\nJune 8, 1925  Court upholds the conviction, 7–2.',
 blurb:'Gitlow was arrested in November 1919, tried in early 1920, and sentenced to prison. His lawyers, including the future ACLU figure Walter Pollak, appealed through New York’s courts and then to the U.S. Supreme Court. They argued that the First Amendment protected his writing, and that it applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment. The Supreme Court heard the case in 1923 and again in 1924, and decided it on June 8, 1925. The path from a local arrest to a national constitutional ruling shows how a single case can change law.',prompt:'Why does it matter that the case went from state court to the Supreme Court?',related:['g4-01','g4-07'],gq:[1]});
a('g4-05',4,1,'document','The First Amendment',{kind:'primary',f:['Bill of Rights - DPLA - a65f6d0b6210c18d17c596908ebcf270 (page 1).jpg'],creator:'First Congress',date:'1791',origin:'Bill of Rights, National Archives',context:'The Bill of Rights as sent to the states in 1789 and ratified in 1791.',relevance:'Protects speech and press.',cite:'gitlow-1925',
 doc:'FIRST AMENDMENT (excerpt)\n\n“Congress shall make no law … abridging the freedom of speech, or of the press; or the right of the people peaceably to assemble …”',
 blurb:'The First Amendment begins with the words “Congress shall make no law.” For well over a century, it was understood as a limit on the federal government only. States could regulate speech and press as long as they followed their own constitutions. That meant that in 1919, a New York court could punish a pamphlet in ways Congress could not. The key question in <i>Gitlow</i> was whether the Constitution protected speech against states too, and the answer lay in another amendment, which you will find next door.',prompt:'Whom does the text of the First Amendment limit?',related:['g4-06','g4-08'],gq:[1]});
a('g4-06',4,1,'document','The Fourteenth Amendment, Due Process Clause',{kind:'primary',f:['14th Amendment Pg1of2 AC.jpg'],creator:'Congress; National Archives',date:'1868',origin:'Washington, D.C.',context:'The Fourteenth Amendment, ratified after the Civil War.',relevance:'The route by which federal protection reaches state governments.',cite:'gitlow-1925',
 doc:'FOURTEENTH AMENDMENT, SECTION 1 (excerpt)\n\n“… nor shall any State deprive any person of life, liberty, or property, without due process of law …”',
 blurb:'The Fourteenth Amendment was written after the Civil War to protect the rights of formerly enslaved people from state governments. Its Due Process Clause says that no state may deprive any person of life, liberty, or property without due process of law. The word “liberty” opened a door. If “liberty” includes freedom of speech, then states, like the federal government, must respect it. In <i>Gitlow</i>, the Supreme Court walked through that door, though it did not use it to free Gitlow himself.',prompt:'How could the word “liberty” in this clause connect to the First Amendment?',related:['g4-05','g4-08'],gq:[1]});
a('g4-07',4,2,'document','Gitlow v. New York, 268 U.S. 652 (1925)',{kind:'primary',creator:'U.S. Supreme Court; opinion by Justice Edward T. Sanford',date:'8 June 1925',origin:'Washington, D.C.',context:'The majority opinion was 7–2. Justices Holmes and Brandeis dissented. The excerpt below is from the public-domain opinion.',relevance:'The Court upheld the conviction, ruling that speech encouraging violent revolution threatens public safety.',cite:'gitlow-1925',
 doc:'EXCERPT (majority opinion)\n\n“… we may and do assume that freedom of speech and of the press — which are protected by the First Amendment from abridgment by Congress — are among the fundamental personal rights and ‘liberties’ protected by the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment from impairment by the States.”',
 blurb:'Justice Sanford’s opinion did two things at once. First, it said that the Court would assume freedom of speech and press are among the “liberties” protected from state impairment by the Fourteenth Amendment. Second, it held that New York could still punish Gitlow, because a state may suppress speech that threatens to overthrow organized government by unlawful means. In dissent, Justice Holmes, joined by Justice Brandeis, argued that the manifesto posed no clear and present danger. The vote was 7–2. Gitlow lost his case, but the principle he argued won.',prompt:'How can a court rule against a person and still strengthen the right he claimed?',related:['g4-08','g4-09'],gq:[1]});
a('g4-08',4,3,'diagram','How incorporation works',{kind:'secondary',ev:1,chart:'incorporation',creator:'Museum curators',date:'—',origin:'Diagram',context:'A simple diagram of how a federal right reaches state governments.',relevance:'The First Amendment’s speech protection applies to states through the Due Process Clause.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'Incorporation is the process by which the Supreme Court has applied parts of the Bill of Rights to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment’s Due Process Clause. Think of it as a pipeline. The First Amendment, written to limit Congress, enters the pipeline when the Court decides that the right is part of “liberty.” It then limits state laws too. The Court did this one right at a time, which is why the doctrine is called selective incorporation. <i>Gitlow</i> was the first step for free speech.',prompt:'Why might the Court have chosen to incorporate rights one at a time?',related:['g4-09','g4-06'],gq:[1]});
a('g4-09',4,3,'chart','Incorporation timeline',{kind:'secondary',ev:1,chart:'timeline-inc',creator:'Museum curators, from secondary sources',date:'1925–1968',origin:'Timeline',context:'Selected cases in which the Court applied rights to the states.',relevance:'<i>Gitlow</i> began selective incorporation, which expanded federal protection of individual rights against state interference.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'After <i>Gitlow</i> in 1925, the Court applied other rights to the states one case at a time. <i>Near v. Minnesota</i> (1931) protected the press against prior restraint. <i>De Jonge v. Oregon</i> (1937) protected peaceable assembly. <i>Everson v. Board of Education</i> (1947) applied the Establishment Clause. <i>Mapp v. Ohio</i> (1961) excluded illegally seized evidence, <i>Gideon v. Wainwright</i> (1963) guaranteed a lawyer to poor defendants, and <i>Duncan v. Louisiana</i> (1968) guaranteed jury trials in serious cases. Each step grew from the idea that began in 1925.',prompt:'How does one decision lead to many later ones?',related:['g4-08','g4-07'],gq:[1]});
a('g4-10',4,0,'newspaper','Red Scare context',{kind:'primary',f:['South Bend News-Times November 8 1919.jpg'],creator:'<i>South Bend News-Times</i>',date:'8 November 1919',origin:'South Bend, Indiana',context:'A front page from the days of the first major Palmer raids, the same period in which Gitlow was arrested.',relevance:'Links the case to the fear of radicals in Gallery II.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'Gitlow was arrested in November 1919, the same month as the first major Palmer raids. This front page from South Bend, Indiana, shows how those raids were covered nationally. Notice the language used for the people arrested. When a society is afraid, courts, police, and juries can be influenced by that mood. The Gitlow trial, the Sacco and Vanzetti trial, and the Palmer Raids all came from the same atmosphere, which is why this exhibit links Gallery II, Gallery III, and Gallery IV.',prompt:'How might a climate of fear influence what judges and juries do?',related:['g3-04','g2-04'],gq:[1,2]});

/* ---------- Gallery V ---------- */
a('g5-01',5,0,'poster','Temperance poster',{kind:'primary',f:['Temperance poster promoting the prohibition of alcohol (22040690650).jpg'],creator:'Temperance movement publisher',date:'early 1900s',origin:'United States',context:'One of a set of lithographic posters used by temperance groups.',relevance:'The prohibition movement.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'Temperance reformers argued that alcohol destroyed families, wasted wages, and filled jails. The movement was led by women’s groups such as the Woman’s Christian Temperance Union and by the Anti-Saloon League, which became one of the most effective pressure groups in American history. Posters like this one used pictures and slogans to move public opinion. The movement began in the 1800s as a moral cause and grew into a national political force by 1917, when war, anti-German feeling, and progressive reform all pushed in the same direction.',prompt:'What emotions does the poster aim to arouse?',related:['g5-02','g5-06'],gq:[1]});
a('g5-02',5,0,'document','The 18th Amendment',{kind:'primary',creator:'Congress; ratified by the states',date:'ratified 16 January 1919; effective 1920',origin:'Washington, D.C.',context:'The only amendment that created a national ban on a product, later repealed.',relevance:'The constitutional ban.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 doc:'EIGHTEENTH AMENDMENT, SECTION 1\n\n“After one year from the ratification of this article the manufacture, sale, or transportation of intoxicating liquors within, the importation thereof into, or the exportation thereof from the United States … for beverage purposes is hereby prohibited.”',
 blurb:'The 18th Amendment, ratified in January 1919 and effective one year later, banned the manufacture, sale, and transportation of intoxicating liquors for beverage purposes. Notice what it did not ban: drinking itself, or possession in the home. Wartime moral enthusiasm helped it pass, as did the framing of grain conservation as a patriotic duty and the villainizing of German-American brewers. Congress and the states approved it by large margins, which shows how strong the reform movement was in 1917 and 1918.',prompt:'Why might a ban on sale be easier to pass than a ban on drinking?',related:['g5-03','g5-04'],gq:[1]});
a('g5-03',5,0,'document','The Volstead Act',{kind:'primary',f:['Act of October 28, 1919 (Volstead Act) - NARA - 299827.jpg'],creator:'U.S. Congress (Andrew Volstead, sponsor)',date:'28 October 1919',origin:'Washington, D.C.',context:'The National Prohibition Act, passed over President Wilson’s veto.',relevance:'The enforcement law.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'The Volstead Act, passed over President Wilson’s veto in October 1919, set out how Prohibition would be enforced. It defined an “intoxicating” beverage as one containing more than one-half of one percent alcohol, which was far stricter than many expected. It allowed exceptions for medicine, religion, and some industrial uses, and these loopholes were widely exploited. It also created a federal Prohibition Bureau, but the bureau was small, underfunded, and often poorly paid. Strong rules with weak enforcement turned out to be a recipe for trouble.',prompt:'What happens when a law is strict but the agency enforcing it is small?',related:['g5-02','g5-08'],gq:[1]});
a('g5-04',5,0,'poster','Grain-conservation poster',{kind:'primary',f:['Food will win the war WWI yiddish poster 1917.jpg'],creator:'U.S. Food Administration',date:'1917',origin:'United States',context:'A Food Administration poster in Yiddish urging immigrants to save wheat for the Allied war effort.',relevance:'Conserving grain was framed as a patriotic duty during the war.',cite:'foner-2019',
 blurb:'“Food will win the war” was the slogan of the Food Administration, led by Herbert Hoover. It asked households to save wheat, meat, and sugar so that more could be sent to Europe. Posters were published in many languages. Brewing and distilling used grain, so wartime laws limited them, and supporters of Prohibition argued that making alcohol was unpatriotic. The war thus gave the temperance movement a powerful new argument and helped connect Prohibition to national sacrifice.',prompt:'How did the war give new arguments to an old reform movement?',related:['g5-05','g5-02'],gq:[1]});
a('g5-05',5,0,'cartoon','German-American brewers',{kind:'primary',q:'German American brewers World War I propaganda',creator:'Museum curators (summary)',date:'1917–1918',origin:'United States',context:'Summary panel. Replace with an archival cartoon if you find one in the Library of Congress.',relevance:'German-American brewers were villainized, which fed moral enthusiasm for Prohibition.',cite:'foner-2019',
 doc:'WHY BREWERS WERE TARGETS\n\n• Many big breweries (Pabst, Schlitz, Anheuser-Busch, Miller) had German-American founders.\n• Prohibition supporters said brewers funded pro-German causes.\n• Anti-German feeling made “beer” and “enemy” seem connected.',
 blurb:'The brewing industry in 1917 was strongly associated with German immigrants. When the United States went to war with Germany, temperance groups took advantage. They accused brewers of disloyalty, and Senate hearings in 1918 explored pro-German brewery spending. The accusation was powerful because it joined two campaigns: the war against Germany and the war against alcohol. Many Americans who had opposed Prohibition on grounds of personal liberty hesitated to defend beer makers during wartime.',prompt:'How did wartime politics change the way people judged the beer industry?',related:['g5-04','g3-01'],gq:[2]});
a('g5-06',5,1,'evidence','Why supporters wanted it',{kind:'secondary',ev:1,creator:'Museum curators, from secondary sources',date:'1900s–1919',origin:'Evidence panel',context:'The aims of Prohibition supporters.',relevance:'Aims: eliminate alcohol abuse, reduce domestic violence, improve workplace productivity, lower crime, and eliminate saloons.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 doc:'FIVE AIMS OF SUPPORTERS\n\n1. Eliminate alcohol abuse\n2. Reduce domestic violence\n3. Improve workplace productivity\n4. Lower crime\n5. Close the saloons',
 blurb:'Supporters were not simply killjoys. Many were women who saw the toll of drunkenness on household budgets and safety, and employers who wanted a steadier workforce. Reformers believed that saloons, which were often tied to political machines and gambling, fed poverty and crime. Progressive-era optimism said that law could improve society. So Prohibition promised five big benefits: less alcohol abuse, less domestic violence, higher productivity, less crime, and the end of the saloon. Judge the policy by those goals, and then compare them with what happened.',prompt:'Which of the five aims seems most reasonable, and which seems hardest to achieve by law?',related:['g5-07','g5-01'],gq:[1]});
a('g5-07',5,1,'evidence','Why opponents resisted',{kind:'secondary',ev:1,creator:'Museum curators, from secondary sources',date:'1919–1933',origin:'Evidence panel',context:'Arguments made against Prohibition.',relevance:'Arguments against, entered from sources.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 doc:'ARGUMENTS AGAINST\n\n• Personal liberty: government should not control private habits.\n• Enforcement: too costly and too hard to carry out.\n• Revenue: the government loses tax income.\n• Culture: many immigrants and city residents saw drinking as normal.',
 blurb:'Opponents, called “wets,” argued that Prohibition violated personal liberty and ignored the habits of millions of people. In cities, immigrant communities treated wine and beer as part of meals, weddings, and religious life. Business owners worried about lost jobs and taxes. Some critics predicted that the law would be impossible to enforce, and they were partly right. Over the 1920s, groups like the Association Against the Prohibition Amendment gathered wealthy and middle-class support for repeal.',prompt:'Which opposition arguments were practical and which were about principle?',related:['g5-06','g5-11'],gq:[1,2]});
a('g5-08',5,2,'photo','A Prohibition raid',{kind:'primary',f:['Prohibition agents destroying barrels of alcohol (United States, prohibition era).jpg'],creator:'Unknown',date:'1920s',origin:'United States',context:'Agents pouring out confiscated liquor.',relevance:'Enforcement difficulties.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'Spectacular raids made the news, but they were the tip of the iceberg. The Prohibition Bureau had a few thousand agents to police a continent, with borders, coasts, and big cities full of customers. Photographers loved scenes of axes and spilled barrels. The reality was that supply kept pace with demand: liquor came by ship from Canada and the Caribbean, from industrial alcohol that was redistilled, and from home stills. The scenes of destruction were symbolic, but the difficulty of enforcement was real.',prompt:'Why did dramatic raids not solve the problem?',related:['g5-09','g5-11'],gq:[1]});
a('g5-09',5,2,'photo','The speakeasy',{kind:'primary',q:'speakeasy Prohibition',creator:'Museum curators (summary)',date:'1920s',origin:'Cities across the United States',context:'Summary panel in place of a single photograph; no verified period photograph was available.',relevance:'Illicit drinking establishments thrived in an unregulated trade.',cite:'century-1999',
 doc:'ABOUT SPEAKEASIES\n\n• Entry often required a password or a card.\n• Owners paid protection to police or gangsters.\n• In New York, thousands of speakeasies operated.\n• Women drank in public more than they had in saloons.',
 blurb:'Saloons were closed, but drinking moved underground. Speakeasies, named for the quiet needed to enter, spread from basements to hotel back rooms to apartment parlors. Many required a password or an introduction. Some hid alcohol in teapots and coffee cups. Because the trade was illegal, there were no inspections, no age limits, and no way to complain about bad liquor. Owners paid off police and gangsters, and the money fed corruption. The Blind Pig, the museum’s hidden club, recreates one, with non-alcoholic props only.',prompt:'What problems appear when an activity is banned but still popular?',related:['g5-10','g5-11'],gq:[1]});
a('g5-10',5,2,'newspaper','Al Capone and Chicago',{kind:'primary',f:['Al Capone in 1930.jpg'],creator:'Chicago Bureau (Federal Bureau of Investigation)',date:'1930',origin:'Chicago, Illinois',context:'Capone was a Chicago crime boss who built his empire on bootlegging and racketeering in the 1920s. He was convicted of tax evasion in 1931.',relevance:'Bootlegging empires earned billions of dollars through illegal distribution and racketeering.',cite:'century-1999',
 blurb:'Al Capone became the most famous gangster of the 1920s. His organization supplied Chicago’s speakeasies, bribed officials, and fought rivals in violent turf wars, including the St. Valentine’s Day Massacre in 1929. Across the country, bootlegging empires like his earned billions of dollars through illegal distribution and racketeering. Prohibition did not invent organized crime, but it gave it a vast, untaxed market with customers who were mostly law-abiding citizens. Capone was finally convicted in 1931, not for bootlegging but for income tax evasion.',prompt:'How did a ban on alcohol create a business opportunity for criminals?',related:['g5-09','g5-11'],gq:[1]});
a('g5-11',5,2,'cartoon','Corruption and disrespect for law',{kind:'primary',f:['Too Many Holes to Watch-August 26, 1923 cartoon by Edwin Marcus.png'],creator:'Edwin Marcus, <i>The New York Times</i>',date:'26 August 1923',origin:'New York',context:'A cartoon on how difficult it was to enforce Prohibition against so many sources of supply.',relevance:'Corruption spread through police departments, courts, and federal agents. Millions of citizens routinely broke the law.',cite:'foner-2019',
 hot:[['The “holes”','Each hole stands for a way liquor reached drinkers, such as smuggling, home production, and loopholes.']],
 blurb:'Cartoonists commented on Prohibition from the start. This 1923 cartoon in the <i>New York Times</i> shows the frustration of enforcement: too many holes to watch. When a law is widely ignored, the damage is not limited to the law itself. Police officers and agents took bribes, courts were swayed, and ordinary citizens who bought a drink became lawbreakers. Millions of otherwise law-abiding Americans came to see the rules as a joke. That erosion of respect for the law is one of the most important unintended consequences of Prohibition.',prompt:'Why does widespread lawbreaking weaken respect for other laws?',related:['g5-10','g5-08'],gq:[1]});
a('g5-12',5,1,'chart','Lost tax revenue',{kind:'secondary',ev:1,chart:'revenue',creator:'Museum curators, from secondary sources',date:'1920–1933',origin:'Chart (qualitative)',context:'A qualitative chart: arrows show direction only. Exact figures are omitted until verified in a cited source.',relevance:'Governments lost millions in alcohol taxes while enforcement spending rose.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 blurb:'Before 1920, taxes on alcohol had been a major source of government income, especially before the federal income tax grew. When Prohibition closed the legal trade, those taxes disappeared. At the same time, the government had to pay for enforcement, courts, and prisons. As the Great Depression began, the case for repeal grew stronger, because legal alcohol could bring in revenue and jobs. This chart shows the direction of change: tax revenue down, enforcement spending up. The Policy Simulation lets you test that trade-off.',prompt:'Why did the Depression strengthen the argument for repeal?',related:['g5-13','g5-07'],gq:[1]});
a('g5-13',5,3,'document','The 21st Amendment, 1933',{kind:'primary',q:'Repeal of Prohibition 1933',creator:'Congress; ratified by state conventions',date:'5 December 1933',origin:'Washington, D.C.',context:'The only amendment ratified by state conventions instead of legislatures; the only one that repealed another.',relevance:'Repealed Prohibition.',cite:'henretta-2014',
 doc:'TWENTY-FIRST AMENDMENT, SECTION 1\n\n“The eighteenth article of amendment to the Constitution of the United States is hereby repealed.”',
 blurb:'The 21st Amendment, ratified on December 5, 1933, repealed the 18th. It remains the only amendment that undid another. It was ratified through state conventions rather than legislatures, a way to bypass the dry lobby in statehouses. Public opinion had shifted for many reasons: crime, corruption, lost revenue, and Depression-era hunger for jobs and taxes. The story of Prohibition is often told as a failed experiment. A fairer way to say it is that the policy had real aims, but the costs and unintended consequences came to outweigh them in the eyes of most Americans.',prompt:'What does repeal tell you about how Americans can correct their own mistakes?',related:['g5-12','g5-02'],gq:[1]});
a('g5-14',5,2,'film','Temperance (student parody)',{kind:'secondary',parody:1,video:'assets/video/temperance-parody.mp4',poster:'assets/video/temperance-poster.jpg',creator:'Myra, Anish, and Mishra (filmed); Anish (edited), as credited on the film’s title card',date:'2026',origin:'The Roaring Society project team',context:'A short black-and-white <b>parody of Prohibition-era temperance crusaders and speakeasies</b>, made by students for this museum. It is <b>not</b> a 1920s source.',relevance:'A modern, playful take on the hidden-bar culture of Prohibition and the stern reformers who tried to stamp it out.',mla:'Myra, Anish, and Mishra, filmers. Anish, editor. <i>Temperance</i> (student parody). The Roaring Society, 2026.',
 blurb:'This short film is a <b>parody of speakeasies and the temperance movement</b>. When the 18th Amendment took effect in January 1920, drinking did not stop. It moved out of saloons and into hidden, illegal bars called speakeasies, where entry often required a password or a card and owners paid off police or gangsters. Temperance reformers, who had campaigned against alcohol for decades, were easy to caricature as stern enforcers, and Prohibition became a favorite target of 1920s cartoonists and comedians. A parody like this exaggerates the secrecy and the self-righteousness to make a serious point lightly: a law that tried to end drinking by banning it created a whole hidden world. Treat it as a modern secondary source. It shows how people today picture the 1920s, not what speakeasies were really like.',prompt:'Why do speakeasies and temperance crusaders still make good comedy, and what does a parody leave out?',related:['g5-09','g5-08']});

